#!/usr/bin/env tssl

# https://tssl.yarb00.dev

!TooSimpleScriptingLanguage 0.7

# Lines starting with `#` are comments. They are ignored by the interpreter.
# `!TooSimpleScriptingLanguage VERSION` is a language version declaration. It must be present once, and only once, before any non-comment statements (comments are allowed before it).
# `COMMAND NAME>` or `my COMMAND NAME>` is a command call (instruction) without arguments.
# `COMMAND NAME> arg1; arg2; ...; argN;` or `my COMMAND NAME> arg1; arg2; ...; argN;` is a command call (instruction) with arguments.

# Too Simple Scripting Language is indentation insensetive. All lines are trimmed (of leading and trailing whitespace) before parsing the file.
# Instructions are separated by line breaks. Line breaks DO matter.

# Valid characters for identifiers (variables, commands, labels): [A-Za-z0-9_ ] (latin letters, numbers, underscores and spaces).
# Identifiers are case insensetive. They are also trimmed before processing (`   print line  ` is the same identifier as `print line`, but `print   line` is different).

# Built-in commands are called directly by their name, while user-defined commands must be prefixed with `my`.
# Variables starting with an underscore are built-in. User-defined variables cannot start with an underscore.
# Since variable creation and variable assignment are the same thing in TSSL, you also cannot mutate the built-in variables, only read them or unset (delete) them.

# Backslashes are escaped as `\\`. Semicolons are escaped as `\;`. String interpolation syntax is `\(VARIABLE NAME)`. Other sequences starting with a backslash are invalid.
# String interpolation can be nested. If variable `arr 2` is set to "hi", and variable `i` is set to "2", then `\(arr \(i))` is expanded as: `\(arr \(i))` => `\(arr 2)` => `hi`.
# String interpolation syntax, and other escape sequences can only be used inside arguments. In other places they are treated as raw text with no special meaning.
# When expanding string interpolation, the interpreter works with arguments as separate isolated string objects.
# String interpolation cannot change the program structure: script's source code is already parsed by that point. String interpolation only works within the string.

# Traditional GOTO exists in a form of labels, which are defined on their own line as `@LABEL NAME`, and the `jump> LABEL NAME;` command.
# You can safely jump to any existing label in the script from any line, above or below.

# User-defined commands, unlike built-in commands, are just glorified GOTOs, and passed arguments (if any) are written to variables `_0`, `_1`, `_2`, etc.
# The total argument count is written to `_argument_count` (equals "0" if no arguments are passed).
# Fallthrough is not allowed: user-defined commands must call `stop>`, `stop> MESSAGE;`, `jump> LABEL NAME;` or another user-defined command before they end (`end command>`).

# Define a command named "main menu".
command> main menu;
	# Print the passed string to STDOUT as it is (without a line break): "[whoami/greet/exit]: ".
	print> [whoami/greet/exit]: ;
	# Read a line from STDIN and save it to the variable named "option".
	ask line> option;
	# Trim the value of "option" and save it back.
	trim whitespace> \(option); option;

	# If value of "option" is "whoami", call the "display user" command.
	if equals> \(option); whoami;
		my display user>
	end if>

	# If value of "option" is "greet", call the "greet" command.
	if equals> \(option); greet;
		my greet>
	end if>

	# If value of "option" is "exit", exit.
	if equals> \(option); exit;
		stop>
	end if>

	# Otherwise, ask again.
	my main menu>
end command>

# Define a command named "display user".
command> display user;
	# Run the "whoami" system executable and pause execution of the script until it exits.
	execute wait> whoami;

	# If "whoami" exited with a non-zero code, let user know.
	if not equals> \(_last_execute_status); 0;
		print line> An error occurred (code: \(_last_execute_status)).;
	end if>

	# Then ask for the input again.
	my main menu>
end command>

command> greet;
	print> Your name: ;
	ask line> name;
	print line> \(name), hi!;
	my main menu>
end command>

print line> Hi! I'm a script that uses TSSL v0.7!;
# The "print line" command called without arguments prints an empty line.
print line>

# Start the main loop.
my main menu>
